19039  When this button is lit up, the puzzle won't be solved until the selected part is located within the area defined by the "positioning rectangle." You create the "positioning rectangle" by clicking and dragging the cursor from top-left to bottom-right of the area you wish to define as the part's "solved" position.
19038  When you choose "Delay," I'll run your puzzle for you. Click the left mouse button when you want the game to start checking for the solution.\n\nUse this "Delay" button to keep players from solving the puzzle too soon. For instance, let's say your puzzle goal says, "Don't let any of the balloons pop." Without programming a delay, the puzzle will automatically be solved, because balloons never start off popped. But if you put a tack where the balloon will bump it in a couple of seconds, you can use the delay to hold off solution-recognition until the balloon is popped. Then the player must figure out a way to keep the balloon from hitting the tack.
19037  When this button is lit up, the puzzle won't be solved until the selected part is located within the area defined by the "positioning rectangle." You create the "positining rectangle" by dragging the cursor from top-left to bottom-right of the area you wish to define as the part's "solved" position.
19036  When you choose "Delay," I'll run your puzzle for you. Click the left mouse button when you want the game to start checking for the solution.\n\nUse this "Delay" button to keep players from solving the puzzle right away. For instance, let's say your puzzle goal says, "Don't let any of the balloons pop." Without programming a delay, the puzzle will automatically be solved, because balloons never start off popped. But if you put a tack where the balloon will bump it in a couple seconds, you can use the delay to hold off solution-recognition until the balloon is popped. Then the player must figure out a way to keep the balloon from hitting the tack.
